/* SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
   SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

   This plugin's panels' layout, as a stylesheet on disk rather than as `SetStyle` calls in the
   middle of the code that builds the tree — the first half of
   https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/881, which is doc 36 § P4's markup debt. The layer stack
   panel's fifty-two came first; the two paint panes' and the brush column's nine followed with
   https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/1106, and are the last block in this file.

   ⚠ **Layout only, and every colour is somebody else's.** `ControlTheme`, `AdvancedTheme` and
   `EditorTheme` are already in the editor's document and already style every control this panel is
   made of; what was inline was the *flex box* — which direction a container stacks in, which one
   grows, and how wide the preview column is. Those are exactly the declarations a reader of
   `LayerStackView` had to reconstruct from three lines at each of nine call sites.

   ⚠ **What is deliberately NOT here: anything the panel changes while it is running.** A `display`
   that is toggled from `Show` — the empty state, the message block, a fill row that only exists for
   a `Constant` source — stays an inline write in C#, because an inline style is a cascade origin
   above this sheet and the two would be arguing rather than layering. What this file gives such an
   element is its *initial* value, which is what the constructor used to write. The `padding-left`
   that indents a nested row stays too: it is `depth × 12px`, and a rule per depth is not a rule.

   ⚠ **The containers are tags and every typed control is a class**, which is
   https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/1071. `rows.Add("layer-stack-row")` builds an element
   whose *tag* is `layer-stack-row` — an element type nothing else styles — so the rules below are
   type selectors, the same shape `NodeGraphTheme.vcss` uses for `graph-note`. A *control* cannot be
   named that way: `Add<Slider>("layer-stack-opacity")` renames the element out of `ControlTheme`'s
   `slider` rule and out of its focus ring, so every `Add<T>` in the view passes its name as a class
   and keeps the tag the control answers to. ⚠ Nothing in this file changed for that, and the reason
   is worth writing down: this sheet had **never** selected a control. Every rule here is a flex box
   on a container, so the twenty-eight renamed elements were invisible to it — the sheet that lost
   them is `ControlTheme.vcss`, which this plugin does not own. */

/* ⚠ **The ladder, restated.** `ControlTheme.vcss` explains it; the statement has to be here too
   because `CascadeLayers.Declare` fixes a layer's position the first time anything names it, so the
   order belongs to whichever sheet a document loaded first rather than to any one file. */

@layer base, components, utilities;

@layer components {

/* ── The panel's two columns ────────────────────────────────────────── */

/* The whole view is a row: rows on the left, the baked picture on the right, and — when the host
   paints — the brush inspector as a third column `PaintBrushInspector` builds into the same root. */
layer-stack { display: flex; flex-direction: row; flex-grow: 1; }

/* The left column takes the room the fixed-width preview leaves. */
layer-stack-rows { display: flex; flex-direction: column; flex-grow: 1; }

/* ⚠ `flex-grow` on the list and on nothing else in the column. The binding row, the actions row,
   the legend and the message block are each as tall as their content; the list is what absorbs a
   resize, which is the one of the five an artist scrolls. */
layer-stack-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; flex-grow: 1; }

/* Which set, which model, which of its meshes — one decision read left to right. */
layer-stack-binding { display: flex; flex-direction: row; }

/* Add a layer, and what kind. */
layer-stack-actions { display: flex; flex-direction: row; }

/* ⚠ Hidden until there is something to say — a heading with nothing under it reads as "nothing was
   checked", and the ordinary case is a stack with nothing wrong. ⚠ No `flex-grow`, deliberately:
   the list of rows keeps the space and this grows only as far as it has messages.

   ⚠ **The one `display` toggle in this panel that is a rule rather than a `SetStyle`, and the port
   is what made that possible.** The paragraph at the top of this file says a `display` written at
   run time has to stay inline, because an inline style is a cascade origin above this sheet and the
   two would argue rather than layer. That is an argument against *writing a style*, not against
   toggling — and `LayerStackChrome.vxml` writes a class instead, which is in this sheet's own
   origin. So both states live here and the specificity of the second is what decides. The other
   thirteen toggles are still `SetStyle`, because the elements they are on are still built in C#. */
layer-stack-messages { display: none; flex-direction: column; }
layer-stack-messages.shown { display: flex; }

/* ⚠ A fixed width rather than a fraction. The picture is the thing being judged, so it keeps its
   size when the rows grow — and the brush column beside it keeps the picture's width when it
   appears, which is why it is added last. */
layer-stack-preview { display: flex; flex-direction: column; width: 280px; }

/* ⚠ A sibling of the layout rather than a child of it, because the empty state is shown by hiding
   that layout — a message inside the thing being hidden is a message nobody sees. Shown by `Show`. */
layer-stack-empty { display: none; }

/* ── The rows ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/* Every row of the list is a horizontal strip, whatever it is a row of: a layer, a layer whose id is
   ambiguous, a fill source, one channel of a fill, or an entry of a mask. The indent that says which
   is nested under which is `padding-left`, written in C# because it is `depth × 12px`. */
layer-stack-row,
layer-stack-fill-row,
layer-stack-fill-channel,
layer-stack-filter-row,
layer-stack-knob-row,
layer-stack-mask-row { display: flex; flex-direction: row; }

/* One tick per channel of the set, inside the layer's own row. */
layer-stack-channels { display: flex; flex-direction: row; }

/* ⚠ **Class selectors, and the only ones in this file.** The four components of a fill colour are
   `NumericInput`s since https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/1004, and they were the first
   controls in the panel to be named by class rather than by tag — the convention #1071 then applied
   to the other twenty-eight. The width is all this sheet has to say about them: four unconstrained
   fields in one row would each ask for their content and push the texture box off the end. */
.layer-stack-fill-red,
.layer-stack-fill-green,
.layer-stack-fill-blue,
.layer-stack-fill-alpha { width: 56px; flex-shrink: 0; }

/* A knob row is a filter's or a mask effect's declared port, drawn from the node type's own
   declaration (https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/1086). The label is fixed so a column of them
   reads as a column, and the field takes the fill row's width for the same reason it does: several
   unconstrained numeric fields in one row each ask for their content. */
.layer-stack-knob-label { width: 96px; flex-shrink: 0; }
.layer-stack-knob-value { width: 56px; flex-shrink: 0; }

/* ── The paint panes ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/* ⚠ **Both panes and the brush column, in one pass, because it was one gap and not three.**
   https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/1106 says the 2D pane's status line was already themed
   while the 3D pane's was not; ⚠ that is wrong and worth recording, because it is the kind of claim
   a fix gets written around. **No** `.vcss` in this repository named a `paint-` anything before
   this block: both panes and `PaintBrushInspector` styled their own hosts with `SetStyle`, and the
   three of them are here together.

   ⚠ **The three declarations on a pane's root are the ones from
   https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/886**, and only two of them do anything. An element no
   stylesheet mentions takes CSS's initial `flex-direction: row`, so the *column* is what decides
   that the title sits above the picture rather than beside it — and `flex-grow` is what gives the
   viewer inside a height at all, since `image-view { flex-grow: 1 }` in `AdvancedTheme.vcss` can
   only grow inside a parent that has room. A root without them is the strip-down-the-left that
   issue's memory names. `display: flex` is stated for the same reason the layers panel states it:
   it is what the constructor wrote, and this file's job is to be able to say what it was. */
paint-mesh,
paint-uv { display: flex; flex-direction: column; flex-grow: 1; }

/* The symmetry picker and its label, above the picture. ⚠ `flex-shrink: 0` is the whole of why this
   is a rule rather than nothing at all, and it is `image-view-bar`'s reason exactly: the viewer
   below declares `flex-grow: 1` on a `flex-basis` of nought, so in a short pane the only thing with
   anything to give up is this strip — the picker squashes to nothing and a pane that grew a control
   appears not to have. `flex-direction: row` is the initial value and is stated rather than
   relied on. */
paint-mesh-bar { display: flex; flex-direction: row; align-items: center; flex-shrink: 0; gap: 6px; }

/* Fixed, so the picker beside it starts in the same place whatever the word is in — the reason the
   knob rows above give, one panel across, and the reason it matters more here is that
   `Strings` can change the word without the panel being rebuilt. */
.paint-mesh-symmetry { flex-shrink: 0; }
paint-mesh-symmetry-label { width: 72px; flex-shrink: 0; }

/* The sentence under each picture. ⚠ `flex-shrink: 0` for the bar's reason and not for the text's:
   a status line that wrapped to two lines in a short pane would be squashed to one and a half by
   the viewer's `flex-grow`, and half a line of text is unreadable rather than merely small. */
paint-mesh-status,
paint-uv-status { flex-shrink: 0; }

/* ⚠ The brush column's fixed width, for `layer-stack-preview`'s reason: it is added last, into the
   layers panel's own root, and a column that took a fraction would change the width of the picture
   beside it every time the paint verb was toggled. */
paint-brush { display: flex; flex-direction: column; width: 220px; }

/* The sentence under the imported-alpha box — https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/1090. ⚠ It
   takes `flex-shrink: 0` for the two panes' status lines' reason and one more of its own: it is the
   only element in this column whose text is a *whole sentence* rather than a word and a number, so
   in a 220px column it is the one rule here that has more than one line to lose. Everything about
   how it reads is `EditorTheme`'s, as this file's opening paragraph requires. */
paint-brush-note { flex-shrink: 0; }

}
